# November 19, 2026: a first scatter of places across Manitoba, which had one place (the Old Pinawa Dam). Region mb, the
# thinnest on the map, claimed with tools/choose.py --claim --thinnest. Sources opened in this session: the Manitoba
# Historical Society's "Historic Sites of Manitoba" page for each place (mhs.mb.ca, read with tools/page.py --all; each
# gives a site coordinate); Canadian Register of Historic Places statements (historicplaces.ca) and the Province's heritage
# site pages (gov.mb.ca/chc/hrb); Wikipedia as raw text (tools/wiki_raw.py); and the pages of whoever runs each place
# (Parks Canada, Manitoba Parks, Seven Oaks House Museum, Friends of Dalnavert, Inglis Grain Elevators, Immaculate
# Conception parish at Cooks Creek).
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
def MHS(t,s): return ['Manitoba Historical Society: '+t,'https://mhs.mb.ca/docs/sites/'+s+'.shtml']
def HP(t,i): return ['Canadian Register of Historic Places: '+t,'https://www.historicplaces.ca/en/rep-reg/place-lieu.aspx?id=%d'%i]
BATCH={'key':'mb_xums','label':'A first scatter across Manitoba','added':'2026-11-19 19:21'}
PLACES=[
# ---- Winnipeg ----
N('trappist-monastery-ruins-st-norbert','Trappist Monastery Ruins (Our Lady of the Prairies)','Winnipeg (St. Norbert)','mb','worship','ruins',49.75708,-97.15445,'site','public','walk','burned','1903–1983',
  'Five Trappist monks from the Abbey of Bellefontaine in France came to St. Norbert in 1892 at the invitation of the parish priest, Monsignor Ritchot, and Archbishop Taché, and founded the monastery of Our Lady of the Prairies by the LaSalle River. The Romanesque Revival church went up in 1903–04 and the monastic wing in 1905, and the monks ran a self-sufficient farm with dairy barns, a cheese factory, bakery, forge, sawmill and cannery. Urban sprawl drove them to a new site near Holland in 1978, and in 1983 fire gutted the empty church and residential wing.',
  'The stabilised shell of the church and monastic wing, fenced against vandalism, with interpretive signs along paths through landscaped grounds. Beside it stands the 1912 guesthouse, built on the foundations of the first 1892 monastery; it escaped the 1983 fire and is now the St. Norbert Arts Centre. The ruins are the stage for Shakespeare in the Ruins.',
  'A French Trappist abbey on the prairie, burned out five years after the monks left.',
  'Trappist Monastery Provincial Heritage Park, run by Manitoba Parks: in St. Norbert just south of the Perimeter Highway, west off Pembina Highway (PTH 75) just north of the LaSalle River. The ruins themselves are fenced; view them from the paths. Park hours not given on its page (not checked). A mapped lot on Des Ruines du Monastère Street is about 80 m northwest.',
  [MHS('Trappist Monastery Ruins and Guesthouse','trappistmonastery'),L('Manitoba Parks: Trappist Monastery Provincial Heritage Park','https://gov.mb.ca/sd/parks/park-maps-and-locations/central/trappist.html')],
  th=[49.75761,-97.15528],thn='Lot on Des Ruines du Monastère Street (OpenStreetMap)',brick='?'),
N('st-boniface-cathedral-facade','St. Boniface Cathedral façade (ruins of the 1908 basilica)','Winnipeg (St. Boniface)','mb','worship','ruins',49.88930,-97.12240,'exact','view','walk','burned','1906–1968',
  'The fifth cathedral on this site facing the Red River, begun in 1906 to a design by Marchand and Haskell of Montreal, was finished in 1908 on a steel frame from Dominion Bridge and was the best example of French Romanesque architecture in Manitoba. Its first church, a log building, was put up by Father Provencher in 1818. A fire on July 22, 1968 gutted the 1908 church, destroying the rose window, the 1860 bells and the parish records; only the façade, the sacristy and the walls survived. A smaller cathedral by Étienne Gaboury was built behind the old façade and blessed in 1972.',
  'The stone façade of the 1908 basilica, standing in front of the 1972 cathedral, which takes in the old walls and sacristy. The tombs of the bishops of Saint-Boniface lie in the façade. A bronze plaque of 1944 near the old main entrance honours the Métis leader Louis Riel; a small plaque at the cemetery\'s northwest corner, at Taché Avenue and Avenue de la Cathédrale, lists all five cathedrals. Interior of the new cathedral not checked.',
  'A cathedral front with nothing behind it but sky and a newer church.',
  'Active cathedral of the Archdiocese of Saint-Boniface at 190 Avenue de la Cathédrale; the façade and cemetery are seen from the grounds. The cathedral\'s own page could not be read, so service and opening times are not checked. No lot was seen on the aerial beside it; park on the street.',
  [MHS('St. Boniface Cathedral','stbonifacecathedral'),W('St. Boniface Cathedral')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('seven-oaks-house-winnipeg','Seven Oaks House (Inkster House and store)','Winnipeg (West Kildonan)','mb','landmark','standing',49.93011,-97.11674,'exact','open','drive','','1826–1853',
  'Seven Oaks House, the Inkster family\'s two-storey, nine-room house, is the oldest home in Winnipeg. It was begun in 1851 of oak logs hewn seven inches square and pegged at the joints, was halted by the great Red River flood of 1852, which stood four feet deep on the property, and was finished in 1853. The Inksters lived in it until 1912, when it passed to the City of Winnipeg; it opened as a museum in 1958. Beside it, the Inkster store, a two-room cottage built between 1826 and 1831 and turned into a general store and post office around 1853, is the oldest surviving building in Winnipeg and a rare piece of early Red River frame building.',
  'The log house on its original stone foundations, furnished as the Inksters\' home with some original pieces, and the Red River frame store, in McGowan Park with much of the old homestead still open ground. The second floor is up stairs only. Guided tours by donation during open hours.',
  'Winnipeg\'s oldest house and its even older store, both on their first foundations.',
  'A City of Winnipeg museum run as Seven Oaks House Museum, 50 Mac Street in McGowan Park. Open Victoria Day to Labour Day; admission by donation (suggested $5). Free parking in its own lot (not mapped in OpenStreetMap). Hours from the museum\'s visiting page, read November 2026, when it was closed for the season until spring 2027.',
  [MHS('Inkster House / Seven Oaks House Museum','sevenoakshousemuseum'),L('Seven Oaks House Museum: visiting information','https://www.sevenoakshouse.ca/visiting-information.html'),W('Seven Oaks House Museum')],
  brick='no',hours='Victoria Day to Labour Day: Wednesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('dalnavert-museum','Dalnavert (Macdonald House)','Winnipeg (Downtown)','mb','landmark','standing',49.88599,-97.14183,'exact','open','walk','','1895',
  'Built in 1895 on Hudson\'s Bay Company Reserve land to a design by Winnipeg architect Charles H. Wheeler, this Queen Anne Revival house was the home of Hugh John Macdonald, a premier of Manitoba and son of Sir John A. Macdonald. It was later cut up into apartments, then restored in the early 1970s by the Manitoba Historical Society to look as it did when new. A visitors\' centre by Wins Bridgman was wrapped round its east side in 2004–05, and the Society handed it to the Friends of Dalnavert in 2014. It is a National Historic Site (1990).',
  'A red-brick house with round-arched windows and a long wooden veranda (Wikimedia photograph), furnished inside for the late Victorian years, shown by guided tour; the second and third floors are up stairs only, and a virtual tour covers them for those who cannot climb.',
  'A premier\'s Victorian house, saved from the rooming-house years.',
  'Run by the Friends of Dalnavert, 61 Carlton Street. Admission charged (adults $10 on its page); tours booked through its site. Closed on several holidays (New Year\'s Day, Good Friday, July 1, September 30, Thanksgiving Sunday, Remembrance Day, Christmas and Boxing Day). Free visitor parking in its lot north of the visitors\' centre (weekdays only the stalls marked Visitor). Hours from its page, read November 2026.',
  [MHS('Macdonald House / Dalnavert Museum','dalnavert'),L('Dalnavert Museum: hours and admission','https://www.friendsofdalnavert.ca/hours-and-admission'),W('Dalnavert')],
  brick='yes',hours='Wednesday to Sunday; September to May noon to 4 p.m., June to August 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
# ---- The lower Red River ----
N('lower-fort-garry','Lower Fort Garry','St. Andrews','mb','fort','standing',50.11208,-96.93167,'exact','open','walk','forts','1830–1846',
  'The Hudson\'s Bay Company built this stone fort on the west bank of the Red River for Governor George Simpson between 1830 and 1846. It was the supply and transport centre of the lower Red River Settlement, linked by schooner to Norway House, and its farm fed the boat brigades. Treaty No. 1, the first of the Numbered Treaties, was signed here on August 3, 1871. After 1870 it served as a penitentiary and as the first training base of the North West Mounted Police; from 1913 to 1962 it was the Motor Country Club, and the company gave it to Canada in 1951.',
  'One of the finest groups of early stone buildings in western Canada, inside limestone walls with four corner bastions: the Big House (1830–32, the governor\'s residence), the Fur Loft and Saleshop (1830–31) and the matching Warehouse (about 1838), the Men\'s House (1852) and the 1885 doctor\'s office. Parks Canada says some historic buildings are closed until further notice. Interiors as shown on the summer programmes; not checked further.',
  'The stone fort where Treaty 1 was signed.',
  'Lower Fort Garry National Historic Site, Parks Canada, 5925 Highway 9, about 30 minutes north of downtown Winnipeg. Admission paid at the Visitor Reception Centre; free parking in its lot. The grounds are open all year; the buildings and the centre open with summer programmes. Hours from Parks Canada\'s visit pages, read November 2026 (its hours page answered 404).',
  [W('Lower Fort Garry'),MHS('Lower Fort Garry','lowerfortgarry'),L('Parks Canada: Lower Fort Garry, plan your visit','https://www.parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/mb/fortgarry/visit'),L('Parks Canada: Visitor Reception Centre','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/mb/fortgarry/visit/centre')],
  th=[50.10982,-96.93642],thn='Visitor Reception Centre lot (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no',hours='Grounds open all year; Visitor Reception Centre and buildings late May to early September (some buildings closed until further notice)'),
N('st-andrews-on-the-red','St. Andrew\'s on the Red Anglican Church','St. Andrews (Lockport)','mb','worship','standing',50.06666,-96.97618,'exact','view','drive','','1845–1849',
  'Designed by Archdeacon William Cockran and built in 1845–49 of limestone from the banks of the Red River under the mason Duncan McRae, it served retired Hudson\'s Bay Company men and English-speaking mixed-blood families of the settlement. It is the oldest stone church in western Canada still in parish use. The churchyard holds many Hudson\'s Bay Company officers and early settlers, among them Captain William Kennedy, Cockran himself and Isaac Cowie; a stone arch of 1922 lists the parish\'s First World War dead.',
  'Rough limestone walls with smooth-cut trim, a square tower with a wooden belfry and spire, and pointed windows, some with their original rolled glass. Inside, by the Register\'s account: a west gallery with intact box pews and carved woodwork, 1890s pews in the nave, plank floors, a nave ceiling framed like an upturned York boat, and the old pulpit and altar rail. When the public may go in was not checked.',
  'The oldest stone church in the West still holding services.',
  'An active Anglican parish church at 3 St. Andrews Road at River Road, south of Lockport; the churchyard is behind a stone wall. No parish page was found, so service times are not checked. A small mapped lot is just west of the church. St. Andrew\'s Rectory National Historic Site (Parks Canada, free) is across the road.',
  [MHS('St. Andrew\'s-on-the-Red Anglican Church and Cemetery','standrewsanglican'),HP('St. Andrew\'s on the Red Anglican Church',5389)],
  th=[50.06656,-96.97680],thn='Small lot west of the church (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no',hours='?'),
N('st-peter-dynevor-old-stone-church','St. Peter Dynevor "Old Stone Church"','East Selkirk (St. Clements)','mb','worship','standing',50.18250,-96.83987,'exact','view','drive','cemeteries','1853–1854',
  'Built in 1853–54 by the mason Duncan McRae under Archdeacon William Cockran, of stone quarried from the Red River bank, it replaced an 1836 church a little to the south. It served St. Peter\'s, the Saulteaux and Cree farming settlement begun here in the 1830s, the first such Anglican mission in western Canada, and was the home church of Chief Peguis, who helped the first Selkirk settlers survive. The reserve was surrendered and its people moved away in the early 1900s. Restoration in 2002–03 turned up Indigenous artifacts centuries old under the floor.',
  'A long, low Gothic Revival church of roughly dressed limestone under a cedar-shake roof, with a stone chancel and vestry, an 1904 wooden belfry and octagonal spire with a Celtic cross, pointed windows with wooden tracery, and inside hand-hewn pews and a carved pulpit and altar (from the Register statement; not seen). The cemetery holds more than 3,000 graves, Chief Peguis\'s among them.',
  'Chief Peguis\'s church, by the river where the mission farmed.',
  'Anglican, used in summer: the St. Peter Dynevor congregation worships here in the summer months and at its 1937 church across the river (1087 Breezy Point Road) from September to June. Summer service times not checked. Stone Church Road, north of East Selkirk; a mapped lot is about 80 m north.',
  [MHS('St. Peter Dynevor Anglican Church and Cemetery','stpeterdynevor'),HP('St. Peter, Dynevor',6940),MHS('St. Peter Dynevor Anglican Church (1937)','stpeterdynevorstandrews')],
  th=[50.18325,-96.83979],thn='Lot on Stone Church Road (OpenStreetMap)',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('immaculate-conception-cooks-creek','Immaculate Conception Ukrainian Catholic Church and Grotto','Cooks Creek','mb','worship','standing',50.00392,-96.77323,'exact','open','drive','','1930–1952',
  'The "prairie cathedral" was designed by the priest-architect Philip Ruh, who worked on it beside his parishioners from 1930 until it was finished in 1952: a brick church in the shape of a cross, 140 feet long, 100 feet wide and 110 feet high, with nine domes and seats for 434. He began the grotto beside it, a concrete copy of the Lourdes grotto, in 1954; after his death in 1962 the Knights of Columbus finished it by 1970. Ruh is buried in the nearby cemetery. The church is a National Historic Site (1997).',
  'The nine-domed church, its walls finished in pale yellow with grey-blue trim (Wikimedia photograph), its interior shown in season, and the concrete grotto and the cemetery beside it. The church was restored under the Manitoba Prairie Churches Project.',
  'A nine-domed cathedral on the prairie, built by its own congregation over 22 years.',
  'Active Ukrainian Catholic parish, 68003 Provincial Road 212. Tours May long weekend to the end of September, $5 a person; Divine Liturgy on Sundays at 10:30 a.m. in English. A mapped lot is about 75 m east. Hours from the parish\'s page, read November 2026.',
  [MHS('Immaculate Conception Ukrainian Catholic Church, Grotto, and Cemetery','ukrainiancatholiccookscreek'),L('Immaculate Conception Ukrainian Catholic Church, Cooks Creek','https://immaculatechurch.ca/'),W('Church of the Immaculate Conception (Cooks Creek, Manitoba)')],
  th=[50.00394,-96.77218],thn='Church lot on Zora Road (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no',hours='Tours May long weekend to end of September, Saturday and Sunday noon to 6 p.m. Divine Liturgy Sundays 10:30 a.m.'),
# ---- The southeast and the Mennonite reserve ----
N('old-st-michaels-gardenton','Old St. Michael\'s Ukrainian Orthodox Church','Gardenton (Stuartburn)','mb','worship','standing',49.07164,-96.74058,'exact','view','drive','cemeteries','1899',
  'Consecrated on October 14, 1899, St. Michael\'s was the first permanent Ukrainian church built in Canada, raised by settlers from northern Bukovyna who came to the Gardenton area after 1896. A Russian Orthodox mission served it until the parish joined the Greek Orthodox Church of Canada in 1922. A new St. Michael\'s was built in Gardenton village in 1935, and since then the old church has held services only two or three times a year, with an annual pilgrimage since 1970.',
  'The church, renovated in the early 2000s under the Manitoba Prairie Churches Project, with the cemetery beside it and provincial (1986) and national (1987) plaques. The Historical Society calls its interior decoration and furnishings Bukovynian pioneer craftsmanship; when it opens apart from the pilgrimage was not checked.',
  'Canada\'s first Ukrainian church, opened two or three times a year.',
  'Owned by the parish; seen from the grounds, in the RM of Stuartburn near Gardenton. Opening and pilgrimage dates not checked. No mapped parking; park on the church grounds or the road.',
  [MHS('Old St. Michael\'s Ukrainian Orthodox Church and Cemetery','stmichaelsukrainianorthodox2')],brick='?'),
N('neubergthal-street-village','Neubergthal Mennonite Street Village','Neubergthal (Rhineland)','mb','town','standing',49.07498,-97.48222,'area','view','drive','','1876',
  'Founded in 1876 by related Mennonite families on the open plains of southern Manitoba, Neubergthal is laid out in the old Strassendorf way: long narrow farmsteads behind fences along one tree-lined street, with the arable fields and pasture once held in common. Several traditional Mennonite housebarns, house and barn under one roof, still stand. It was made a National Historic Site in 1998. The Neubergthal Heritage Foundation keeps the H. F. Hamm House and the Friesen Interpretive Centre.',
  'The single tree-lined street with the housebarns behind their fences; the Friesen housebarn interpretive centre, the Hamm House, the Herdsman\'s House and the restored Klippenstein housebarn are municipal heritage sites. Interiors and opening times not checked.',
  'A Mennonite street village still farmed in the shape it was laid out in 1876.',
  'A living village: the farmsteads are private homes, seen from the street. The Heritage Foundation\'s tour and visit pages answered 404, so tour times are not checked. No mapped parking. The pin is the village street.',
  [MHS('Neubergthal Mennonite Street Village','neubergthalstreetvillage'),W('Neubergthal')],brick='?'),
# ---- The southwest and the west ----
N('criddle-vane-homestead','Criddle/Vane Homestead (Aweme)','Aweme (Glenboro-South Cypress)','mb','landmark','ruins',49.70875,-99.60202,'site','public','walk','burned','1882–1960',
  'Percy Criddle, his wife and children, and Elise Vane and her children came from England to the Carberry sand hills in 1882 and tried to make a genteel English landscape there, with a tennis court and a golf course. Percy and his sons were naturalists; Norman Criddle\'s insect collecting here made Aweme the longest-studied single insect site in Manitoba. The families left in 1960, the Province bought the homestead in the 1970s and made it a provincial heritage park in 2004. The main house was destroyed in a suspected arson fire on June 25, 2014.',
  'Under a kilometre of self-guiding Homestead Trail loops round the site of the house, the small frame building that was the first entomological field station in western Canada, the family cemetery, old foundations, a garden and what is left of the tennis courts and golf course, with interpretive signs. (The park brochure, printed in February 2014, predates the fire.)',
  'An English family\'s prairie estate, with western Canada\'s first insect laboratory.',
  'Criddle/Vane Homestead Provincial Heritage Park, Manitoba Parks, about 40 km southeast of Brandon. A provincial park vehicle permit is needed all year. Hours not given on the park pages (not checked). No mapped parking: the pin is the homestead.',
  [MHS('Criddle-Vane Homestead','criddlevanehomestead'),L('Manitoba Parks: Criddle / Vane Homestead Provincial Heritage Park','https://www.gov.mb.ca/nrnd/parks/park-maps-and-locations/western/criddle.html'),L('Criddle/Vane Homestead park brochure (2014)','https://www.gov.mb.ca/sd/pubs/parks-protected-spaces/park_info/criddle_vane_brochure.pdf'),W('Criddle/Vane Homestead Provincial Park')],brick='?'),
N('beautiful-plains-county-court-neepawa','Beautiful Plains County Court Building','Neepawa','mb','civic','standing',50.22832,-99.46447,'exact','view','walk','','1884',
  'Built in 1884 by Gossil and Dykeman to a design by the Winnipeg architect C. Osborn Wickenden, it held the county court, the jail, the municipal offices and a theatre. It is the second-oldest court house standing on the Prairies and one of the few substantial public buildings left in Manitoba from the 1880s. It is a National Historic Site and a provincial heritage site.',
  'A two-storey building of buff brick from Portage la Prairie with limestone trim on a granite-boulder foundation: a pedimented entrance tower with a wrought-iron balustrade, round-arched windows, a bull\'s-eye window and decorative brick chimneys, and a well-lit courtroom upstairs (Register statement). Grounds with two war memorials and a fountain. Interior not checked.',
  'The second-oldest court house on the Prairies.',
  'At 282 Hamilton Street in downtown Neepawa; the Beautiful Plains Archives use the same address. Seen from the street; when the public may go in was not checked. Street parking on Hamilton Street.',
  [MHS('Beautiful Plains County Court House','beautifulplainscountycourt'),HP('Beautiful Plains County Court Building',3544)],
  brick='no'),
N('riding-mountain-east-gate','Riding Mountain East Gate Registration Complex','Riding Mountain National Park','mb','landmark','standing',50.68325,-99.55525,'exact','public','drive','','1933–1934',
  'Riding Mountain National Park had three rustic gates, the South (1931), East (1933) and North (1936). The East Gate and Norgate Road were built by local men on the Depression relief programme, and the registration building and two staff cabins went up in 1933–34. It is the only one of the three gates left, and a National Historic Site (1992).',
  'Two log-and-stone pavilions, one each side of the road, topped by cupolas and joined by a roofed sign across PTH 19; nearby the gatekeeper\'s residence and the Whirlpool warden\'s station.',
  'The last of Riding Mountain\'s three log gates.',
  'Parks Canada, on PTH 19 at the park\'s east edge. A park vehicle permit is needed to enter Riding Mountain National Park. OpenStreetMap maps an East Gate lot about 70 m north, not seen under the trees on the aerial.',
  [W('Riding Mountain Park East Gate Registration Complex'),MHS('Riding Mountain East Gate Entrance Complex','ridingmountaineastentrance'),L('Parks Canada: East Gate, plan your visit','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/mb/eastgate/visit')],
  brick='no'),
N('inglis-grain-elevators','Inglis Grain Elevators','Inglis','mb','industry','standing',50.94428,-101.24902,'site','open','walk','','1922',
  'When the Canadian Pacific reached Inglis in 1922, four of these five wooden grain elevators were up by the end of that year, and the nearby town of Asessippi was soon abandoned. They were little changed in seventy years of use while the number of grain companies in Manitoba fell from 67 to eight. When the rail line was abandoned the companies gave them to a local group, which restored them as a museum. It is the last row of country elevators in Canada and a National Historic Site.',
  'Five restored wooden elevators in a row on the old CPR track bed, including two former Reliance Grain Company elevators restored with a 2003 gift; plaques of 2000 and 2002.',
  'The last elevator row on the prairies.',
  'Owned by a local group that keeps them as a museum, on Railway Avenue, Inglis. Admission charged; guided tours by reservation all year. Hours from its page, read November 2026. No mapped parking.',
  [W('Inglis Grain Elevators National Historic Site'),MHS('Inglis Grain Elevators National Historic Site','ingliselevators'),L('Inglis Grain Elevators National Historic Site','https://ingliselevators.com/')],
  brick='no',hours='May long weekend to September long weekend, 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; guided tours by reservation all year'),
# ---- The north ----
N('christ-church-the-pas','Christ Church Anglican (Devon Mission)','The Pas','mb','worship','standing',53.82703,-101.25619,'exact','view','walk','cemeteries','',
  'This frame church succeeds the 1840s church of the Devon Mission, founded in 1840 and the first self-supporting Anglican mission in the Canadian northwest. Much of its woodwork was carved by the Richardson party, which wintered here in 1846–47 on its way to search for Sir John Franklin, and was moved over from the first church. It also keeps four painted canvas panels sent from England by the Reverend John West for the Reverend Henry Budd\'s mission, linked to the writing of Cree in the Latin alphabet. When the present building went up was not found in the sources read.',
  'A modest Gothic Revival church with lancet windows, wooden buttresses, a small belfry and a rounded apse; inside the carved woodwork from the 1840s church, the painted Cree panels and a stained-glass window to the steamboat captain Horatio Hamilton Ross. Henry Budd is buried in the cemetery, with a provincial plaque in front. When the public may go in was not checked.',
  'Furniture carved by men hunting for Franklin.',
  'An active Anglican church owned by the parish, 2 Saskatchewan Crescent. Service times not checked. Mapped lots on Edwards Avenue and 1st Street West, about 100 m away.',
  [MHS('Christ Church Anglican and Cemetery','christchurchanglicanthepas'),L('Manitoba Municipal Heritage Site No. 382: Christ Church','https://www.gov.mb.ca/chc/hrb/mun/m382.html')],
  th=[53.82618,-101.25544],thn='Lot on 1st Street West (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no',hours='?'),
N('prince-of-wales-fort','Prince of Wales Fort','Churchill','mb','fort','ruins',58.79722,-94.21253,'site','boat','boat','forts','1731–1771',
  'The Hudson\'s Bay Company began a log post here in 1717 and renamed it Prince of Wales Fort in 1719. The stone star fort that replaced it took from 1731 to 1771 to build at the mouth of the Churchill River: outer walls 6.5 m high and 11 m thick, four bastions and forty cannon. It is the oldest building west of Ontario and the northernmost fort in North America, and a National Historic Site since 1920; Cape Merry and Sloop Cove were added in 1933.',
  'The massive stone walls and bastions with the earthen rampart between the outer and inner walls and the stone ravelin guarding the main gate; a 1936 plaque to Samuel Hearne on the southwest bastion.',
  'A stone star fort on Hudson Bay, 40 years in the building.',
  'Parks Canada, across the Churchill River from Churchill, which is reached only by VIA Rail or air. Parks Canada does not provide transport: licensed commercial boat operators run to the fort in season. Daily fees; it is polar bear country. Parks Canada\'s page said in November 2026 that the fort was closed for the season; opening dates not checked.',
  [W('Prince of Wales Fort'),MHS('Prince of Wales Fort','princeofwalesfort'),L('Parks Canada: Prince of Wales Fort, plan your visit','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/mb/prince/visit')],brick='no'),
N('york-factory-depot','York Factory Depot','York Factory (Hayes River)','mb','fort','standing',57.00271,-92.30443,'exact','boat','boat','forts','1831–1957',
  'The Hudson\'s Bay Company\'s posts at the mouths of the Hayes and Nelson rivers worked for 275 years, and York Factory, headquarters of its Northern Department from 1821 to 1873, was its main trading hub in the early 19th century. After the floods of 1788 the post moved half a mile upstream to this site. Of as many as 50 buildings, the largest survivor is the Depot, built in 1831–38: 100 by 105 feet, mostly two storeys with a three-storey lookout, the oldest and largest wooden building on permafrost in Canada. The company closed the post in 1957 and gave it to the government in 1968.',
  'The great wooden Depot with its lookout, on the north bank of the Hayes River about 7 miles in from Hudson Bay, with a powder house and cemetery nearby (Manitoba Historical Society pages). Nobody lives there; Parks Canada staff are there in summer.',
  'The oldest wooden building in western Canada, a long way from any road.',
  'York Factory National Historic Site, Parks Canada; open to visits in summer, free admission. No road: charter aircraft, seasonal boat tours from Gillam, or a hard paddle down the Hayes River (register with Parks Canada in Churchill and the RCMP). Polar bear country. Opening dates not checked.',
  [W('York Factory'),MHS('York Factory Depot Building','yorkfactory'),L('Parks Canada: York Factory, plan your visit','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/mb/yorkfactory/visit')],brick='no'),
]
